    <item>
      <title>Bay du Nord oil project clears final regulatory hurdle, awaits Equinor's next move</title>
      <link>https://www.bnnbloomberg.ca/business/company-news/2026/11/20/bay-du-nord-oil-project-clears-final-regulatory-hurdle-awaits-equinors-next-move/</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">48e9161eb4c0ad0cbda17dad</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 21:55:31 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>Equinor’s proposed Bay du Nord offshore oil project has cleared its final regulatory hurdle in Newfoundland and Labrador.</description>
      <dc:creator>The Canadian Press</dc:creator>
      <content:encoded>&lt;p&gt;ST. JOHN’S — Equinor’s proposed Bay du Nord offshore oil project has cleared its final regulatory hurdle in Newfoundland and Labrador. Officials with the federal and provincial governments announced today they had accepted the development’s approval by the Canada-Newfoundland and Labrador Offshore Energy Regulator. Premier Tony Wakeham says the approval was an important milestone ahead of Equinor’s final decision next year on whether it will proceed with the project. He said his government is still considering whether to buy a 10 per cent stake in the $14-billion development. Bay du Nord would be the first deepwater oil development in Canada, and farther from shore than any other oil project on Earth. Norway-based Equinor would drill wells more than a kilometre below the surface of the ocean, in waters about 500 kilometres northeast of St. John’s, N.L. The company expects to extract more than 400 million barrels of oil, with first oil expected in 2031. This report by The Canadian Press was first published Nov. 20, 2026.&lt;/p&gt;</content:encoded>
